<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sylvanus Olympio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1902">1902</a>–<a href="/years/1963">1963</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sylvanus Olympio (1902–1963) was a Togolese businessman and nationalist who led French Togoland to independence. He headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-togolese-unity">Committee of Togolese Unity</a>, became prime minister after UN-supervised elections in 1958 and president of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a> in 1960. He was assassinated in January 1963 in the first military coup in post-independence <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sylvanus-olympio.jpg" alt="Sylvanus Olympio" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ludwig Wegmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sylvanus_Olympio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sylvanus Olympio was born on 6 September 1902 at Kpandu, in the German protectorate of Togoland. He came from an important family of mixed Brazilian and other African descent: his grandfather was the Brazilian trader Francisco Olympio Sylvio, and his uncle Octaviano Olympio, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lome">Lomé</a>, became one of the richest people in the colony. He studied economics at the London School of Economics under Harold Laski. He worked for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unilever">Unilever</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, headed its operations in Togoland by 1929 and by 1938 was general manager of the United Africa Company throughout Africa.</p><p>During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the colony came under the Vichy France government, which treated the Olympio family with suspicion because of its ties to the British. Olympio was arrested in 1942 and held under surveillance at Djougou in French Dahomey. The imprisonment changed his view of the French, and he pushed for independence once the war ended.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a> was a trust territory, and in 1947 Olympio sent the first petition for the resolution of grievances to the United Nations Trusteeship Council. At home he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-togolese-unity">Comité de l'unité togolaise</a>, the main party opposing French control. It boycotted most elections of the 1950s because of heavy French involvement, and French authorities arrested Olympio in 1954 and suspended his right to vote and stand. His petitions led to the elections of 1958, in which his party won every elected position in the national council and he became prime minister. He defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-grunitzky">Nicolas Grunitzky</a> in the presidential vote with more than 90 per cent to become the first president of Togo.</p><p>In foreign policy he drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and other Western countries, and visited President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a> in 1962. Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> were tense, as the two quarrelled over the western part of the former German colony. Domestically he restrained spending and kept the army to about 250 soldiers. After an attempt on his life in 1961, in which Grunitzky's party and the Juvento movement were accused, the opposition was outlawed, and Togo became largely a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shortly after midnight on 13 January 1963, members of the military broke into the house of Olympio and his wife. Before dawn his body was found by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> ambassador, Leon B. Poullada, near the door of the American embassy. Étienne Eyadéma later claimed to have fired the shot. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-grunitzky">Nicolas Grunitzky</a> was installed as president, and Eyadéma took power in 1967.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sylvanus Olympio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Committee of Togolese Unity, led by <b>Sylvanus Olympio</b>, campaigned for independence and Ewe unity, while Nicolas Grunitzky led parties closer to France.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-togoland">French Togoland</a></li><li><blockquote>The Togolese Republic was proclaimed on 27 April 1960, and <b>Sylvanus Olympio</b> became its first president in 1961.</blockquote><a href="/state/togo">Togo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lome">Lomé</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/togo">Togo</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lome">Lomé</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/togo">Togo</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.13,1.22&amp;year=1941" aria-label="Lomé on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4584 1934 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sylvanus Olympio"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5034 2204h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5034 2204h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=6.13,1.22&amp;year=1941">What was here in 1941? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
